format 1
adapter scanner 1
scene: kitchen

target scan-water
name: A glass of water
object: water
references: ref-openstax-chem-2-4
blurb: Every drop is made of the same tiny molecules.
fun_fact: Hydrogen and oxygen can react to form water, and it is the same water in a glass, a lake or a cloud.

composition:
    {"symbol":"H","note":"joined with oxygen"}
    {"symbol":"O","note":"joined with hydrogen"}

target scan-salt
name: Table salt
object: salt
atomdex: ion
references: ref-openstax-chem-2-4
blurb: A shake of salt from the kitchen.
fun_fact: Sodium and chlorine can react to form table salt.

composition:
    {"symbol":"Na","note":"joined to chlorine as ions"}
    {"symbol":"Cl","note":"joined to sodium as ions"}

target scan-banana
name: A banana
object: banana
atomdex: potassium-40
references: ref-epa-radiation-sources
references: ref-hps-natural-radioactivity
references: ref-medlineplus-potassium
blurb: Sweet, soft and a good source of potassium.

fun_fact:
    A tiny part of all natural potassium is radioactive potassium-40, so food with potassium holds a trace of it. So does your own body.

composition:
    {"symbol":"O"}
    {"symbol":"C","note":"in sugars and starch"}
    {"symbol":"H"}
    {"symbol":"K","note":"a mineral found in many foods"}

target scan-rice
name: A grain of rice
object: rice
references: ref-openstax-bio-carbs
blurb: A grain: one of the foods that give you carbohydrates.

fun_fact:
    Glucose, a sugar your body uses for fuel, is made of just carbon, hydrogen and oxygen. Plants store glucose as starch, a chain of glucose molecules.

composition:
    {"symbol":"O"}
    {"symbol":"C","note":"in the sugar chains of starch"}
    {"symbol":"H"}
